# 3 readings by claude-opus-5-5, compared
boethius-3.md:1: argue/readings: S3 is read 2 ways: boethius-3.logic.r2 and boethius-3.logic.r2.2 have "good_ruler ∧ evil_exists ∧ evil_unpunished"; boethius-3.logic.r2.3 has "good_ruler ∧ ◇evil_exists ∧ ◇(evil_exists ∧ evil_unpunished)"; they differ when only "there exists a good ruler of the universe", "evil is at all", "possibly, (evil is at all and evil goes unpunished)" and "possibly, evil is at all" are true, which the second allows
boethius-3.md:1: argue/modal-ambiguous: S3 is read boethius-3.logic.r2 and boethius-3.logic.r2.2 with no modality; boethius-3.logic.r2.3 as what is necessary or possible: its "should" does not say which it means
boethius-3.md:1: argue/answered: S3: answered yes, which keeps boethius-3.logic.r2.3, to: Suppose "there exists a good ruler of the universe", "evil is at all", "possibly, (evil is at all and evil goes unpunished)" and "possibly, evil is at all" are true and "evil goes unpunished" is false. Is S3, "But, lo! herein is the very chiefest cause of my grief--that, while there exists a good ruler of the universe, it is possible that evil should be at all, still more that it should go unpunished.", true then?
boethius-3.md:1: argue/readings: S4 is read 2 ways: boethius-3.logic.r2 and boethius-3.logic.r2.2 have "good_ruler ∧ evil_exists ∧ evil_unpunished → astonishing"; boethius-3.logic.r2.3 has nothing from it; they differ when only "there exists a good ruler of the universe", "evil is at all" and "evil goes unpunished" are true, which the second allows
boethius-3.md:1: argue/answered: S4: answered no, which keeps boethius-3.logic.r2 and boethius-3.logic.r2.2, to: Suppose "there exists a good ruler of the universe", "evil is at all" and "evil goes unpunished" are true and "this of itself deservedly provokes astonishment" is false. Is S4, "Surely thou must see how deservedly this of itself provokes astonishment.", true then?
boethius-3.md:1: argue/readings: S6 is read 2 ways: boethius-3.logic.r2 has "god_omniscient ∧ god_omnipotent ∧ god_wills_good" and "god_omniscient ∧ god_omnipotent ∧ god_wills_good ∧ wickedness_flourishes ∧ virtue_punished → beyond_wonder"; boethius-3.logic.r2.2 and boethius-3.logic.r2.3 have "god_omniscient ∧ god_omnipotent ∧ god_wills_good" and "god_omniscient ∧ god_omnipotent ∧ god_wills_good ∧ wickedness_flourishes ∧ virtue_unrewarded ∧ virtue_punished → beyond_wonder"; they differ when only "wickedness reigns and flourishes", "virtue is trampled under the feet of the wicked and suffers punishment in the place of crime", "God knows all things", "God can do all things" and "God wills only the good" are true, which the second allows
boethius-3.md:1: argue/answered: S6: answered no, which keeps boethius-3.logic.r2, to: Suppose "wickedness reigns and flourishes", "virtue is trampled under the feet of the wicked and suffers punishment in the place of crime", "God knows all things", "God can do all things" and "God wills only the good" are true and "virtue lacks its reward" and "that this should happen cannot be sufficiently wondered at nor sufficiently lamented" are false. Is S6, "That this should happen under the rule of a God who knows all things and can do all things, but wills only the good, cannot be sufficiently wondered at nor sufficiently lamented.'", true then?
boethius-3.logic.r2: argue/readings: what the text leaves unstated is read 2 ways: boethius-3.logic.r2 and boethius-3.logic.r2.2 have nothing from it; boethius-3.logic.r2.3 has "good_ruler ∧ ◇evil_exists → astonishing"; they differ when only "there exists a good ruler of the universe" and "possibly, evil is at all" are true, which the first allows
